def make_report():
    m = D["metrics"]
    section = []
    section.append('''<header><p class="eyebrow">Read-only forensic audit · 21 October 2026</p><h1>Why are the extraction scores low?</h1>
    <p class="lead">The scorer is arithmetically correct. The benchmark mixes genuine Qwen mistakes with an underspecified, open-label agreement task. The current scores cannot by themselves measure absolute factual extraction quality.</p>
    <div class="badges"><span>180 paragraphs · 9 MuSiQue records</span><span>179 valid outputs · 1 failed output</span><span>No new inference</span><span>Original artifacts unchanged</span></div></header>''')
    section.append('<nav><a href="#answers">Findings</a><a href="#diagnostics">Measured diagnostics</a><a href="#examples">Source examples</a><a href="#gold">Reference quality</a><a href="#rankings">Ranked fixes</a><a href="#protocol">Experiment protocol</a><a href="#methods">Audit & sources</a></nav>')
    answers = [
        ("Is Qwen just weak?", "Real weaknesses are visible, but model capacity is not isolated.", "Examples include a plant typed as an animal, nationality typed as an organization, incorrect relation arguments, missing information, and degenerate repeated triples. There is no controlled larger-model or thinking-on comparison here; quantization, decoding, annotation policy and checkpoint all matter."),
        ("Is evaluation too strict?", "Strict label agreement is a major contributor.", "There is no similarity cutoff to lower. Entity matches require normalized surface text AND type; relation matches require both typed endpoints AND the directed relation label. With unconstrained label vocabularies, valid paraphrases, coarse types and inverses can be counted wrong."),
        ("Is evaluation incorrect?", "No arithmetic, ID alignment or frozen-reference error found.", "Independent recomputation matches every stored row. Gold/candidate cohorts, fingerprint, references and source identities validate. Renaming IDs and reordering arrays preserves perfect self-agreement. The important weakness is construct validity: this measures agreement with one annotator's representation, not all supported facts."),
        ("Is gold wrong and Qwen actually good?", "Gold is unreviewed model-generated silver; the reversal claim is not supported.", "All gold mention strings occur in their paragraph bodies. That is reassuring grounding evidence, not semantic verification. The manual examples show both defensible Qwen alternatives and clear Qwen errors. A blind human audit is still required before estimating gold error or ranking absolute quality."),
        ("Are better evaluation methods available?", "Use a suite: strict reproducible agreement plus validated semantic diagnostics.", "Add surface-only and canonical-schema scores, controlled one-to-one overlap alignment, relation evaluation conditional on entity alignment, and blind source-supported fact review. Keep strict results visible. No fuzzy score, embedding similarity or LLM judge alone is a substitute for truth."),
        ("Could extraction improve?", "Yes, there are testable candidates; no gain has been measured yet.", "Start with clear guidelines, a fixed ontology, recorded decoding, a stronger baseline and entity-then-relation extraction. Compare specialized models and task adaptation after an independent reference and held-out development/test split exist."),
    ]
    section.append('<section id="answers"><h2>Answers to the six questions</h2><div class="cards">' + ''.join(
        f'<article><h3>{esc(q)}</h3><strong>{esc(a)}</strong><p>{esc(detail)}</p></article>' for q,a,detail in answers) + '</div></section>')
    section.append(f'''<section class="critical"><h2>Fix the checkpoint choice before testing reasoning</h2>
    <p>The current local <code>qwen3:4b-instruct</code> metadata says <code>general.finetune=Instruct</code>, <code>general.version=2507</code>, and links to the Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 license. Its official card identifies it as <strong>non-thinking only</strong>. Ollama's generic <code>thinking</code> capability is insufficient evidence that the flag changes its learned behavior. {cite('S1')} {cite('S3')}</p>
    <p>The installed <code>qwen3:4b</code> instead identifies <code>Thinking-2507</code> with controls <code>[true]</code>; comparing these two 4B tags changes checkpoint weights. The installed <code>qwen3:8b</code> exposes <code>[false,true]</code> controls and its model card documents both modes. It is the clearest already-installed starting point for a paired mode experiment. {cite('S2')} {cite('S13')}</p>
    <p>Historical run files record model tags and preflight family/quantization, but no immutable weight digest. Current local metadata, including digests, is saved in <a href="model_identity.json">model_identity.json</a>; it does not retroactively prove historical identity. All local model checks were metadata-only.</p></section>''')
    section.append('<section id="diagnostics"><h2>What changes when the matching question changes?</h2><p>Every value below uses the same saved predictions and gold labels. These are exploratory projections, not corrected headline scores, not semantic accuracy estimates, and not model improvements. Removing fields can merge distinct tuples and change denominators.</p>')
    section.append(bars("entity-diagnostics.svg", "Entity micro F1: sensitivity to representation", [
        ("Official: exact normalized surface + type",m["entities_strict"]["f1"],"#2463a6"),
        ("Diagnostic: harmonize CamelCase type formatting",m["entities_format"]["f1"],"#5b7fa6"),
        ("Diagnostic: exact normalized surface only (ignore type)",m["entities_surface"]["f1"],"#007d75")]))
    section.append(bars("relation-diagnostics.svg", "Relation micro F1: successive diagnostic projections", [
        ("Official: typed endpoints + directed relation label",m["relations_strict"]["f1"],"#2463a6"),
        ("Ignore endpoint types; keep direction and exact relation label",m["relations_untyped"]["f1"],"#5b7fa6"),
        ("Ignore types AND relation label; keep directed endpoint pair",m["relations_pairs"]["f1"],"#007d75"),
        ("Ignore types, relation label AND direction; compare unordered pairs",m["relations_undirected"]["f1"],"#824bb0")]))
    names = [("entities_strict","Entities: official"),("entities_format","Entities: type-format diagnostic"),("entities_surface","Entities: surface-only"),
             ("relations_strict","Relations: official"),("relations_untyped","Relations: untyped endpoints + label"),
             ("relations_pairs","Relations: directed pairs only"),("relations_undirected","Relations: unordered pairs only")]
    section.append(table(["Metric / question","TP","FP","FN","Precision","Recall","F1"],
        [[name,*[m[key][count] for count in ("tp","fp","fn")],*[percent(m[key][score]) for score in ("precision","recall","f1")]] for key,name in names]))
    section.append('''<p>Case, Unicode normalization, punctuation/underscores and repeated whitespace already normalize away. <code>PERSON</code> versus <code>Person</code> is not penalized. <code>StreamingService</code> versus <code>STREAMING SERVICE</code> still differs because CamelCase word boundaries are not split by the official scorer. Splitting those type boundaries adds only 0.86 entity F1 points here; this is a minor issue beside type granularity. Doing the same to relation-label formatting adds no matches in the untyped relation diagnostic.</p>
    <p>There are <strong>574 shared mention surfaces with non-overlapping type sets</strong> and <strong>385 shared directed endpoint pairs with disjoint label sets</strong>. These counts show disagreement, not 574 or 385 independently adjudicated mistakes. Conditional relation-label F1 on the 529 shared directed surface pairs is 26.40%; it omits missing/wrong pairs and ignores entity types, so it is not end-to-end performance.</p>
    <p>Gold has 3,308 unique typed entity keys and 3,502 typed relation keys after per-paragraph deduplication. Qwen has 2,025 and 1,511. Only 1,757 of 3,437 distinct gold directed surface pairs have both endpoint surfaces in Qwen; only 501 have both types available. This helps explain the severe multiplication of entity and predicate errors in strict relation scoring.</p>''')
    section.append('<h3>Frequent disagreements</h3><div class="columns">' + table(["Gold type","Qwen type","Paragraph/surface cases"],D["type_confusions"][:10])
                   + table(["Gold label set","Qwen label set","Shared pair cases"],D["relation_disagreements"][:10]) + '</div>')
    section.append('<p>Some label pairs are near paraphrases (LOCATED_IN / IS_IN); some signal direction or semantic errors (OWNED_BY / OWNS on unchanged endpoints). Automatic synonym replacement would incorrectly forgive the latter.</p></section>')
    section.append('''<section id="examples"><h2>Read the underlying facts</h2><p>The 12-paragraph review sample was selected with fixed seed <code>20261021</code>, independent of per-case scores; it is not stratified or a human ground-truth audit. The narrative review is by the assistant, not two blinded annotators. Three explanatory cases below come from this sample; the degenerate NBCSN output is explicitly a targeted outlier. No population error rate is inferred from these examples.</p>''')
    cases = [
        (104,"A correct fact can receive zero relation credit", "Gold links Daniel Bremer Juell —BORN_IN→ Siljord; Qwen uses HAS_BIRTH_PLACE for the same endpoints. Gold uses child→parent CHILD_OF, while Qwen emits parent→child IS_PARENT_OF. Several facts are defensible while exact triples differ. Qwen also omits occupations and other details."),
        (60,"Typed endpoints can reject an otherwise matching relation", "Both use Minsk Region —HAS_ADMINISTRATIVE_CENTER→ Minsk. Gold types Region/City; Qwen types Place/Place, so strict relation matching rejects it. In contrast, Qwen's 1,411,500 —HAS_POPULATION→ Minsk Region reverses the natural subject/object convention and should not be excused as a harmless synonym."),
        (3,"A definite model mistake remains after relaxing evaluation", "The paragraph describes a growing papyrus sprout. Qwen types papyrus sprout as ANIMAL, misattaches other relations, and omits much of the paragraph. The choice Person versus Deity for Osiris is a separate taxonomy issue, not the same kind of error."),
        (168,"A valid JSON response can still be unusable extraction", "Targeted outlier, not part of the seeded sample. The NBCSN prediction contains 99 raw triples but only two distinct surface/label triples: NBCSN —has_name→ July 1, 1995 and NBCSN —has_type→ July 1, 1995. Schema validity and deduplication do not make those facts correct. This motivates a controlled decoding/pipeline test, not a proved cause."),
    ]
    for index,title,note in cases:
        e=D["examples"][index-1]
        section.append(f'<article class="case"><h3>{index}. {esc(title)}</h3><p>{esc(note)}</p><p class="meta">{esc(e["chunk_id"])} · {esc(e["title"])}</p><blockquote>{esc(e["text"])}</blockquote><details><summary>Inspect saved labels and resolved triples</summary>')
        for side in ("gold","qwen"):
            triples=list(dict.fromkeys(tuple(t) for t in e[f"{side}_relations"]))
            section.append(f'<h4>{side.capitalize()}</h4>' + table(["Mention","Type"],[[x["mention"],x["entity_type"]] for x in e[f"{side}_entities"]])
                           + table(["Subject","Relation","Object"],triples))
        section.append('</details></article>')
    section.append('<details><summary>All 12 sampled cases and review notes</summary>'+table(["1-based index","Title","Interpretation"],[[i,D["examples"][i-1]["title"],SAMPLE_NOTES[i]] for i in sorted(SAMPLE_NOTES)])+'</details></section>')
    section.append(f'''<section id="gold"><h2>Is the reference trustworthy?</h2>
    <p>It is a <strong>single-model silver reference</strong>, stored under the historical gold terminology. Structural validation verifies shapes, identity, provenance and IDs, not factual truth, consistent ontology, annotation completeness or an appropriate relation granularity. A lower score against this reference does not prove a lower utility graph.</p>
    <p>There are <strong>395 distinct gold type strings</strong> (167 used once) and <strong>1,368 relation strings</strong> (876 used once), versus 134 and 811 normalized strings in Qwen. The prompt requests consistency but supplies no fixed label inventory or definitions. Gold often extracts concepts, occupations, date nodes and reified events, while Qwen sometimes emits coarser direct facts. Both are plausible representations until guidelines define the task. Gold's density alone does not prove hallucination, and Qwen's sparsity alone does not prove good precision.</p>
    <p>A literal grounding screen found <strong>0/3,589</strong> gold mention occurrences absent from the body, and <strong>18/2,062</strong> Qwen occurrences absent even after normalization (33 fail raw substring matching). These are raw occurrence counts, not deduplicated metric denominators. Title-only NASCAR is one clear candidate violation of the copy-from-body instruction. Grounded words can still have wrong types, arguments or unsupported relations. This screen is not a semantic hallucination rate.</p>
    <p>The seeded review does not establish that the reference is generally wrong or that Qwen performed well overall. It establishes representation ambiguity and concrete Qwen failures. Independent double annotation is the next way to answer the gold-error question. Research on DocRED shows why missing reference facts can distort evaluations, but its error rates cannot be transferred to this dataset. {cite('S8')}</p></section>''')
    section.append(f'''<section><h2>Protocol weaknesses beyond the scorer</h2>
    <ul><li><strong>Mixed generation contracts:</strong> all 180 reference responses are migrated legacy outputs; Qwen has 171 legacy and 9 simplified outputs. Both original legacy source prompts match, but the current mixed candidate is not a clean single-prompt run. Comparing it with a fresh thinking-on run would add a prompt/schema confound. The two subgroups are not randomized, so their similar F1 values do not prove format has no effect.</li>
    <li><strong>Decoding differs from published guidance:</strong> current provider code sends Ollama temperature=0 and overrides the model's local 0.7 parameter. The Instruct card suggests 0.7, top_p=0.8, top_k=20. The code does not record/configure a seed or num_ctx. Exact historical wire settings for every legacy row are unavailable. Test decoding as a separate factor; do not assume it caused the errors. {cite('S1')}</li>
    <li><strong>Context is not established:</strong> maximum body length here is only 1,577 characters, making missing label policy more directly evidenced than severe source truncation. However, instructions, schema, input, generated answer and thinking share runtime constraints. Model-advertised context is not the allocated runtime window. No truncation diagnosis is established from saved data. {cite('S12')}</li>
    <li><strong>One schema failure is not the main cause:</strong> the failed paragraph refers to nonexistent target M20. The official policy makes the whole prediction empty. Even replacing it with perfect gold would move entity F1 from 37.24% to 38.45%, and relation F1 from 2.79% to 4.08%. This optimistic bound is not an observed repair.</li>
    <li><strong>Small, clustered coverage:</strong> 180 unique paragraph texts come from just 9 MuSiQue records selected as a prefix. This is not 180 independent documents. Broad claims need more diverse held-out document groups and uncertainty estimates.</li>
    <li><strong>Retried failures and temperature=0 do not create experimental replicates:</strong> reused successes plus selectively regenerated failures form the present snapshot. Record equal retry policies prospectively, retain all attempts in future runs, and compare independent full runs rather than cherry-picking best retries.</li></ul></section>''')
    section.append(f'''<section><h2>A more informative evaluation suite</h2>
    <ol><li><strong>Keep strict normalized typed micro P/R/F1 as the archival baseline.</strong> Add per-record or macro scores so long dense paragraphs do not wholly dominate. Report raw validity, generation failures, omissions, retry counts, resource use and the exact evaluation population.</li>
    <li><strong>Add surface-only entity matching and controlled type canonicalization.</strong> For future span annotations, report strict span/type and one-to-one overlap F1 separately. Current artifacts lack trusted offsets, so substring matching is not a true span benchmark. Fit alias/boundary rules on separate development material, freeze them and inspect precision/recall changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate relations at multiple levels.</strong> End-to-end strict triples, relation classification on predicted/aligned entity pairs, and a clearly labeled gold-entity oracle answer different questions. Use a fixed ontology or preregistered inverse mapping; keep negation, direction, argument roles and temporal qualifications. A token/embedding match alone cannot distinguish ownership from reverse ownership. CaRB offers ideas for open-text tuple matching but is not a drop-in score for this typed graph representation. {cite('S7')}</li>
    <li><strong>Audit source-supported facts with blinded humans.</strong> Judge each sampled predicted fact for support, argument correctness and scope; annotate missing facts independently for recall. Consider an independent LLM judge for triage only after calibrating it against human decisions. Blind model names and randomize order; document judge disagreement and self-preference risks. Published judge findings concern other tasks, not validated extraction accuracy here. {cite('S10')}</li>
    <li><strong>Quantify uncertainty on paired differences.</strong> Use repeated seeds for sampled decoders and resample common document/record groups, recomputing micro counts per bootstrap draw. With only 9 clusters, intervals are fragile. More clusters are preferable to pretending every mention is independent. This adapts paired bootstrap reasoning from established evaluation work. {cite('S11')}</li>
    <li><strong>Measure downstream utility separately.</strong> Evaluate graph-supported QA/retrieval with a controlled retriever and human-checked facts. Better QA may be useful despite stylistic extraction mismatches, but does not validate every extracted triple or replace the extraction experiment.</li></ol></section>''')
    section.append('<section id="rankings"><h2>Ranked propositions</h2><p>These are informed priorities, not observed treatment effects. Effort assumes one engineer and available hardware; training and human review add resources. “Most improvement” targets genuine extraction quality potential. Measurement/validity-only fixes have no rank in that column or extraction ROI: raising a relaxed score is not an extraction improvement. The highest-ceiling option is not necessarily the best first investment. Professional and academic ranks indicate priority; the protocol below orders implementation dependencies.</p>')
    section.append('<label for="ranking">Sort by </label><select id="ranking"><option value="ease">Easiest to implement</option><option value="impact">Most extraction improvement potential</option><option value="value">Easiest for most extraction improvement</option><option value="professional">Professional recommendation</option><option value="academic">Academic recommendation</option></select><p id="rank-status" aria-live="polite"></p>')
    section.append('<div class="scroll"><table id="proposals"><thead><tr><th>ID / proposition</th><th>Effort / expected benefit</th><th>Ease rank</th><th>Improvement rank</th><th>Effort / improvement rank</th><th>Professional rank</th><th>Academic rank</th></tr></thead><tbody>')
    for p in PROPOSALS:
        attrs=' '.join(f'data-{key}="{p[key] if p[key] is not None else 999}"' for key in ("ease","impact","value","professional","academic"))
        section.append(f'<tr {attrs}><td><a href="#{p["id"]}">{p["id"]}: {esc(p["title"])}</a></td><td>{esc(p["effort"])}<br>{esc(p["gain"])}</td>'
                       + ''.join(f'<td>{p[k] if p[k] is not None else "N/A: measurement / validity"}</td>' for k in ("ease","impact","value","professional","academic"))+'</tr>')
    section.append('</tbody></table></div><h3>First choices under each criterion</h3>'+table(["Criterion","Top choices","Why"],[
        ["Easiest","F1 diagnostics → F5 larger baseline → F10 invalid-output branch","F1 is demonstrated here. F5 uses installed weights. F10 is easy but its upper bound is small."],
        ["Most extraction improvement potential","F9 task adaptation → F4 two-stage extraction → F3 ontology/guidelines","Potential, not measured expected percentage points. F9 has the greatest data/cost uncertainty."],
        ["Easiest for most extraction improvement","F3 ontology/guidelines → F8 decoding/context → F5 stronger baseline","Targets observed failure mechanisms without starting with training; judge on untouched held-out data."],
        ["Professional","F6 reviewed reference → F3 task specification → F4 grounded pipeline","Establish a trustworthy target and implement a maintainable extraction process before spending on adaptation."],
        ["Academic","F2 valid mode intervention → F6 independent labels → F1 complementary metrics","Prioritizes an identifiable reasoning effect, construct validity and transparent reporting."]]))
    for p in PROPOSALS:
        section.append(f'<article class="proposal" id="{p["id"]}"><h3>{p["id"]}. {esc(p["title"])}</h3><p><strong>Implement:</strong> {esc(p["action"])}</p><p><strong>Preserve integrity:</strong> {esc(p["integrity"])}</p><p class="meta"><strong>Evidence and limits:</strong> {esc(p["basis"])}</p></article>')
    section.append(f'<p>Relevant extraction methods: GLiNER {cite("S4")}, GLiREL {cite("S5")}, GoLLIE {cite("S6")}, LoRA {cite("S9")}. Their published results motivate candidates; they are not measurements on your corpus.</p></section>')
    section.append(f'''<section id="protocol"><h2>Recommended next experiment without changing this one</h2>
    <ol><li><strong>Archive the baseline:</strong> keep the two v2 input directories, current scorer, original comparison and this investigation with source hashes. Label these results exploratory agreement against model-generated silver. No source files were changed by this audit.</li>
    <li><strong>Freeze task and development boundaries:</strong> decide the desired graph ontology, span/entity identity rules, inverse relations, negation and treatment of concepts. Tune prompts, decoding, postprocessing and any mappings only on disjoint development documents. Since these 180 passages have been inspected, reserve untouched document groups for confirmatory results.</li>
    <li><strong>Establish independent labels:</strong> double-annotate a reviewed reference and adjudicate. Preserve the old reference and every correction as a versioned dataset; rerun all candidate systems against the new reference rather than retroactively altering old metrics.</li>
    <li><strong>Run a clean factorial comparison:</strong> use a verified dual-mode checkpoint such as the installed 8B. At minimum, run on/off with the same digest, quantization, simplified prompt/schema, entity-extraction mechanism, chosen sampling profile, seed list, context and retry policy. Include 3-5 seeds for sampled decoding. A shared total generated-token budget can be the primary resource control; thinking consumes part of it. Report final-answer/thinking usage and truncation, and separately analyze equal-cost or model-optimized profiles if needed. {cite('S2')} {cite('S3')}</li>
    <li><strong>Use fresh full-run directories:</strong> never mix migrated outputs with newly generated responses within the confirmatory on/off comparison, and never selectively replace unfavorable successes. The current CLI already supports model, think and output choices, but explicit decoding/context metadata and reasoning-output observability require a code extension before the proposed rigorous protocol.</li>
    <li><strong>Report all planned conditions:</strong> strict typed metrics, supplementary aligned metrics, validity, semantic human audit, resource cost and paired uncertainty. Resample common document groups rather than separately sampling the two arms. Add more than the current 9 clusters before making broad generalization claims.</li></ol>
    <p>The recommended fixes are proposals in this report. No extraction reruns, training jobs, model downloads, revised gold labels or changes to the primary scoring implementation were performed.</p></section>''')
    section.append('<section id="methods"><h2>Audit method, reproducibility and limits</h2><ul><li>Validated both v2 runs with existing artifact loaders, then checked the exact cohort, frozen reference and fingerprint match.</li><li>Independently recomputed TP/FP/FN and P/R/F1 from mention text/type and resolved triple endpoints for all 180 rows; agreed with the official scorer and stored scores within 1e-12.</li><li>Renamed all mention IDs and reordered mentions/triples for every reference result. Self-comparison stayed exact, demonstrating IDs and array positions do not enter the semantic keys.</li><li>Computed diagnostic projections by removing fields symmetrically from gold and predictions; preserved per-paragraph set deduplication. Conditional relation-label metrics use only shared directed pairs. These projections are not interchangeable primary metrics.</li><li>Hashed every file in both run directories before and after the audit; all bytes remained identical. Stored source hashes and scoring/provider code hashes in evidence.json.</li><li>Reviewed all 12 fixed-seed sample paragraphs, plus explicitly targeted examples. This is an exploratory assistant review, not a calibrated human audit, a blinded study, an estimated error rate or a causal model-quality comparison.</li><li>Queried localhost /api/version, /api/tags, /api/show and /api/ps for metadata only. No models were loaded or run; /api/ps was empty. Current metadata cannot prove historical run digests.</li></ul>')
    section.append('<p><a href="audit.py">Audit program</a> · <a href="evidence.json">Full evidence and file hashes</a> · <a href="sample_selection.json">Sample selection</a> · <a href="model_identity.json">Current local model identity</a> · <a href="findings.json">Ranked findings data</a></p><pre>.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe runs/entity-extraction-reports/gpt-sol-qwen-investigation/audit.py\n.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe runs/entity-extraction-reports/gpt-sol-qwen-investigation/build_report.py</pre>')
    section.append('<p>Use read access to the v2 run folders. Re-running the builder alone uses the saved evidence snapshot. The artifact fingerprint is <code>'+esc(D['candidate_manifest']['configuration']['gold_fingerprint'])+'</code>. The inspected candidate run ID is <code>'+esc(D['candidate_manifest']['run_id'])+'</code>.</p>')
    section.append('<h3>Primary sources checked 21 October 2026</h3><p>Research provides method-level evidence; implementation ranks and transfer expectations are the analyst’s judgments. Links are optional reading; report content and figures are embedded and work offline.</p><ol class="sources">')
    for ident,title,url,note in SOURCES:
        section.append(f'<li id="{ident}"><a href="{esc(url)}">{ident}. {esc(title)}</a><p>{esc(note)}</p></li>')
    section.append('</ol></section>')
    script = '''const select=document.getElementById("ranking"),body=document.querySelector("#proposals tbody");
    function sortRows(){const key=select.value;Array.from(body.rows).sort((a,b)=>Number(a.dataset[key])-Number(b.dataset[key])).forEach(row=>body.appendChild(row));document.getElementById("rank-status").textContent="Sorted by "+select.options[select.selectedIndex].text+". Rank 1 is highest; N/A means the action changes measurement or validity, not extraction quality.";}
    select.addEventListener("change",sortRows);sortRows();'''
    css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}html{scroll-behavior:smooth}body{margin:0;background:#f3f6fa;color:#20364a;font:16px/1.65 system-ui,Arial,sans-serif}main{max-width:1240px;margin:auto;padding:28px}header{background:#17344d;color:white;border-radius:16px;padding:38px}h1{font-size:40px;line-height:1.15;max-width:900px;margin:12px 0 22px}h2{font-size:27px;margin-top:0}h3{font-size:20px;line-height:1.35}h4{margin-bottom:8px}.lead{font-size:21px;max-width:1000px}.eyebrow{color:#a7dfde;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1.5px;font-weight:700}.badges{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}.badges span{font-size:13px;border:1px solid #7892a7;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:5px}nav{display:flex;gap:15px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:24px 0}a{color:#176999;text-underline-offset:3px}section{background:white;padding:28px;border:1px solid #dce4ec;border-radius:12px;margin:24px 0}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:18px}.cards article{border-top:4px solid #16877e;background:#f5f9fc;padding:18px}.cards h3{margin-top:0}.cards strong{color:#12594f}.critical{border-left:6px solid #c57e19;background:#fff9eb}.scroll{overflow:auto}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;margin:16px 0}th,td{padding:11px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #dce4ec}th{background:#edf3f8}td{min-width:75px}svg{width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:20px 0}.columns{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:20px}.case,.proposal{padding:20px;border:1px solid #dce4ec;margin:20px 0;border-radius:8px;scroll-margin-top:10px}.meta{color:#52677a;font-size:14px;overflow-wrap:anywhere}blockquote{background:#f5f8fb;border-left:3px solid #7189a1;margin:16px 0;padding:15px;white-space:pre-wrap}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:600;color:#176999}code,pre{background:#eaf0f5;padding:2px 5px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere}pre{padding:15px;white-space:pre-wrap}select{font:inherit;padding:8px;max-width:100%;border:1px solid #9aadbf;border-radius:5px}.sources li{margin-bottom:16px}.sources p{margin-top:4px;font-size:14px;color:#52677a}li{margin-bottom:10px}@media(max-width:760px){main{padding:12px}header,section{padding:20px}.cards,.columns{grid-template-columns:1fr}h1{font-size:32px}}@media print{body{background:white}main{padding:0;max-width:none}header{background:white;color:#20364a;border:1px solid #9aadbf}.eyebrow{color:#176999}nav,select,label[for="ranking"],#rank-status{display:none}section{break-inside:auto}svg,.case,.proposal{break-inside:avoid}h2,h3{break-after:avoid}.scroll{overflow:visible}table{font-size:10px}th,td{padding:5px;min-width:0}.cards{display:block}}'''
